{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact head 31ee9b1142ba7e277662245d5a9898e23937c938 for mint lane C, request #9476 (git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5ff58c9a73883c89fc22511d7ebbfa17fbc5ce4d). The combined head resolves review #10284 and includes landed safe-error main df22d7713cc239f96a92f74596ee87509967a99f. All 34 artifacts were individually inspected, with plans/README.md as primary.\n\nThe composed migration retains safe-error migration 2 and puts the due indexes in migration 3. Real-DO controls reopen version-1 and already-version-2 stores, preserve token owners, deadlines, ledger, history and metadata, and confirm both production queries use their indexes without a temporary sort. A partly completed version-2 scrub retains its cursor and finishes through production alarms after restart. Version-2 upgrades do not restart the historical scrub. Founded, gone and unfounded paths preserve their cleanup scheduling and bounded work.\n\nThe merge keeps the adopted mint ownership semantics and the landed confidentiality controls: safe job projection; fixed integration failure metadata; legacy mint adoption with a fixed note; transactionally coupled adoption, marker and removal; bounded transactional scrub rows and cursor; and earliest scrub/founding wake on an unfounded Room. Independent controls pass for 501-row cleanup in 500/1 batches with no idle writes, rollback on cursor-update failure, and rollback/retry of legacy-mint adoption.\n\nArchitecture: this is ordinary Git application implementation (Gitseq layer 1), coordinated through the Workroom application/interpreter and its projections (layers 5–7). It changes none of the kernel, nexus or host-binding contracts (layers 2–4) described in Gitseq's docs/reference/architecture.md. Within Artroom, Git credential ownership/publisher adapters, Room storage/migrations, alarms and check-job dispatch implement the already adopted R-MINT contract. The single ordered migration chain composes the two delivered features without changing that contract. The separate application-declared-acts design does not change this review's scope; the operator's #10285 direction expressly lets lanes already under review finish.\n\nSecurity: no unresolved defect found in the reviewed ownership, deadline, confidentiality or cleanup boundaries. Prior review #10284 already verified the retained source scanner and mint guards using 60 production/committed-test mutations plus two supplemental scanner mutations on e03a9c95; those are prior evidence, not claimed as rerun on this head. This review inspected the merge and changed boundaries, ran the current suites, and independently exercised ten composition mutations. Each loaded and produced a named runtime assertion failure: omit migration-3 indexes; fold the indexes into already-recorded migration 2; restart scrub in migration 3; remove the job-index tie-break column; remove safeJobStatus projection; persist raw integration errors; omit scrub scheduling; and separately omit unfounded scrub due, work or recovery. The folded-into-v2 mutant specifically passes the version-1 upgrade but fails the independent version-2 reopen assertion for missing indexes. Compilation or setup failure is not counted as that proof. Unknown mint effects remain unresolved rather than guessed from inventory, scope or time; the scanner remains syntactic with its documented computed-name limits.\n\nAuthority-bearing chain, verified from the durable record: (1) foundation #1 and the current roster establish planner's operator authority at the commissioning request; (2) #8651 explicitly commissioned the contract decision and lane-edit plan only, while #9476 separately commissioned this implementation after adoption; (3) independent approval #9463 was ratified and the contract landed through receipt #9465 before #9476, satisfying the design commission; (4) the decision chain and current design artifact #9467 remain effective and unretired. Builder's effective promise #9875 binds all 34 artifacts, and the implementing commits carry the request provenance. This approves the implementing head for normal guarded landing, with no deployment or live-provider authority.\n\nSimplification: one ledger replaces duplicated mint lifetime/debt paths; the existing migration chain and shared query constants support upgrades and query-plan proof without a second upgrade mechanism. The obsolete integration minted flag stays removed. The merge retains fixed safe error metadata and the existing bounded scrub rather than adding another cleanup framework. No removable complexity requiring another revision was found.\n\nIndependent validation in fresh disposable clones at the exact head: npm ci; root npm test (2,319 passed, one skipped); root typecheck; Git Workers (11 passed); scratch Git harness typecheck; Room-local Wrangler 4.146 dry-run bundle, all exit 0. After all ten mutations were restored, 105 focused Room tests (including five independent controls) and five scanner tests pass. git diff --check against df22d771 passes; both review clones are clean after removal of the checker-only files. Source, controls, scripts and logs are under /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-c-v4*. No canonical source edit, upload, deployment, provider call or real credential creation was performed. Row-cost repairs, the orphan report's outstanding verification condition and the full application scope remain separate open work.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7121c0e27163eb042245e0ed94f6ccbbcfbe97aa","binding":"assigned","head":"31ee9b1142ba7e277662245d5a9898e23937c938","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:783517291ad8561985233747c05318f49d63a840\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:98f13889578e939bbc365086a9a1794742e80397\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:181d33494c0bffad35b